// Names, sends, and parallel composition — reference solutions
// Sections correspond to the matching chapter in exercises/.

// koan: 03.01 — Address the message
// Send 7 to inbox. The receive below waits for a message on inbox, binds its value as n,
// and prints it.

new inbox, stdout(`rho:io:stdout`) in {
  inbox!(7) |
  for (@n <- inbox) { stdout!(n) }
}

// koan: 03.02 — Two processes can run together
// Run both sends. The parallel composition operator allows both processes to run; their
// printed order is not specified.

new stdout(`rho:io:stdout`) in {
  stdout!(1) | stdout!(2)
}

// koan: 03.03 — Fresh names are distinct
// Make the comparison false by comparing two different fresh names. new creates distinct
// names; * exposes a name as a process value so it can be compared.

new left, right, stdout(`rho:io:stdout`) in {
  stdout!(*left == *right)
}

// koan: 03.04 — A receiver chooses a channel
// Print only 2. Messages on one channel cannot satisfy a receive on a different fresh
// channel.

new left, right, stdout(`rho:io:stdout`) in {
  left!(1) | right!(2) |
  for (@value <- right) { stdout!(value) }
}

// koan: 03.05 — A quoted value can name a channel
// Receive the message sent on the name made by quoting the string score. @ turns a
// process value into a name; equal quoted values identify the same channel.

new stdout(`rho:io:stdout`) in {
  @"score"!(9) |
  for (@n <- @"score") { stdout!(n) }
}

// koan: 03.06 — Doing nothing is a process
// Print 7 without adding another action. Nil is the inactive process; composing it in
// parallel with an action leaves that action alone.

new stdout(`rho:io:stdout`) in {
  stdout!(7) | Nil
}
